1
00:00:00,313 --> 00:00:06,211
The same four deliveries give two results: 66.7% and 50.0%.

2
00:00:06,211 --> 00:00:08,057
Which belongs on the dashboard?

3
00:00:08,057 --> 00:00:11,123
The missing piece is the question we want the number to answer.

4
00:00:12,323 --> 00:00:14,751
Use the invented example on this page.

5
00:00:14,751 --> 00:00:16,961
Four parcels were promised by noon.

6
00:00:16,961 --> 00:00:22,440
At one in the afternoon, two arrived on time, one arrived late, and one is unfinished.

7
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:24,630
Assume the records are complete at that moment.

8
00:00:25,508 --> 00:00:28,295
First, count only completed deliveries.

9
00:00:28,295 --> 00:00:32,471
How many parcels belong in the denominator, the total we divide by?

10
00:00:32,471 --> 00:00:34,116
Make your choice before we count.

11
00:00:39,276 --> 00:00:41,149
Three parcels have arrived.

12
00:00:41,149 --> 00:00:43,775
Keep the late parcel, because it is completed.

13
00:00:43,775 --> 00:00:46,980
Leave the unfinished parcel outside this group.

14
00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:50,624
Two on time out of three completed gives 66.7%.

15
00:00:51,575 --> 00:00:54,404
Now count all deliveries due by noon.

16
00:00:54,404 --> 00:00:58,138
The unfinished parcel was due too, so bring it into the group.

17
00:00:58,138 --> 00:01:00,543
The two on-time parcels stay the same.

18
00:01:00,543 --> 00:01:02,641
Two out of four gives 50.0%.

19
00:01:02,641 --> 00:01:04,294
Same data, different questions.

20
00:01:05,292 --> 00:01:08,470
Delivery Operations chooses the all-due question.

21
00:01:08,470 --> 00:01:12,996
Its recorded definition includes unfinished deliveries, measures at one in the

22
00:01:12,996 --> 00:01:15,443
afternoon, and names the responsible team.

23
00:01:15,443 --> 00:01:19,611
This is metadata: information describing what the measure means.

24
00:01:19,611 --> 00:01:21,539
The full definition is on the page.

25
00:01:22,692 --> 00:01:24,322
Check your understanding.

26
00:01:24,322 --> 00:01:30,570
The definition includes all four deliveries due, but the dashboard shows 66.7%.

27
00:01:30,570 --> 00:01:32,688
Its query ran successfully.

28
00:01:32,688 --> 00:01:35,772
Does that prove the dashboard follows the definition?

29
00:01:35,772 --> 00:01:37,620
Pause and explain your answer.

30
00:01:43,925 --> 00:01:44,659
No.

31
00:01:44,659 --> 00:01:48,956
This calculation removed the unfinished parcel and counted only three.

32
00:01:48,956 --> 00:01:52,726
The recorded rule requires four, giving 50.0%.

33
00:01:52,726 --> 00:01:55,682
A successful query can answer the wrong question.

34
00:01:55,682 --> 00:01:58,163
Compare the filter and denominator with the definition.

35
00:01:59,167 --> 00:02:01,902
Back on the page, open the metadata map.

36
00:02:01,902 --> 00:02:05,382
Find Metric definition, then Example record.

37
00:02:05,382 --> 00:02:09,808
Identify what counts, when it is measured, and which team can explain it.

38
00:02:09,808 --> 00:02:13,848
Then ask: does recording the rule prove the calculation follows it?

39
00:02:13,848 --> 00:02:16,928
Use the page's Check your answer to test your explanation.
